--TEST--
An object whose static type names no class renders through its runtime `toString`, and every spelling of the conversion agrees (`rule:classes/stringable`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`, `rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
class Tag implements Stringable {
    public string $label = "a";
    public function toString(): string { return "<" . $this->label . ">"; }
}
class Plain {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// `rule:types/erased-member-access`: an erased receiver "cannot be checked at compile time at all",
// so the conversion is decided by the concrete instance behind the handle —
// exactly as a property read through the same view is. `mixed` is § 4's third
// trigger and answers the same way.
object $o = new Tag();
Tag $t = new Tag();
mixed $m = new Tag();

// `rule:classes/stringable` makes `as string`, `.` and `echo` one conversion, so what a
// value renders as may not depend on which binding read it. Counted rather
// than read off a line: a spelling that grew an answer of its own fails here
// while still printing plausibly on its own.
int $agree = 0;
if (($o as string) == ($t as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (($m as string) == ($t as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (("" . $o) == ($t as string)) { $agree = $agree + 1; }
if (($o as string) == "<a>") { $agree = $agree + 1; }
echo $agree, "\n";
echo $o, "\n";

// The other side of the same bound: a class the erased view might have held
// but which declares no `toString`. `rule:types/erased-member-access` makes that "a checked,
// catchable throw", never a silent value and never a fatal — and `rule:types/conversion`
// 's `as` either produces a `string` or throws, so both spellings throw
// the same thing.
mixed $p = new Plain();
try {
    echo $p as string;
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
object $q = new Plain();
try {
    echo $q;
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
4
<a>
cannot convert an object of class `Plain` to `string`: it does not implement `Stringable`
cannot convert an object of class `Plain` to `string`: it does not implement `Stringable`
